    excerpt:
      "Salesforce and Anthropic announced Claudeforce this week, and the division of labour in it is unusually explicit: Claude brings the reasoning, Salesforce brings the governed data and the workflows.",
    readTime: "6 min read",
    body: [
      "On 26 August, Salesforce and Anthropic announced Claudeforce, making Claude the default reasoning engine across the Salesforce ecosystem. It ships in both directions: a Salesforce plugin inside Claude with 37 prebuilt sales skills, and Claude inside Agentforce powering the Atlas Reasoning Engine by default.",
      "Salesforce has around $5 billion invested in Anthropic, so the partnership is not a surprise. The framing is what is worth reading, because both companies described the split of responsibilities plainly: Claude brings reasoning and judgement, Salesforce brings the governed data, the business rules, and the trusted workflows.",
      "## That sentence is a strategy",
      "Salesforce is making a bet, and it is a defensive one. It is betting that its data and workflows remain essential even if Claude becomes the interface its customers spend their day inside. Marc Benioff has been publicly answering questions about what people are calling the SaaSpocalypse, which is the worry that agents make application software redundant.",
      "The answer embedded in Claudeforce is that they do not, provided the software owns something the agent cannot replicate. Not the interface, which is exactly what is being conceded. The record, the rules that govern it, and the audit path around both.",
      "It is worth noticing this is a concession dressed as a partnership. Salesforce is allowing that its customers may soon work primarily in someone else's interface. The counterargument it is making is that this does not matter as long as the governed record underneath is theirs.",
      "## Why this matters at a much smaller scale",
      "The same split applies to a one-person product, and it resolves a question a lot of people are stuck on.",
      "If you are building something whose value is that it reasons well about a problem, you are competing with the reasoning layer directly, and that layer is improving monthly, is available to everyone, and is priced by people who can afford to sell it below cost. That is not a position.",
      "If you are building the place where a specific kind of record lives, along with the rules about who may change it, what makes an entry valid, and what the history of it is, you own something the reasoning layer explicitly wants to plug into rather than replace. Salesforce exposes its data to agents through MCP servers, APIs, and command line tools precisely because being the thing agents reach into is a defensible place to be.",
      "## The regulated angle",
      "One further detail from the announcement: Claude is the first model provider fully integrated inside the Salesforce Trust Boundary, which is what makes this usable in regulated industries.",
      "Trust boundaries are not glamorous and they are where a lot of value is going to sit. In any industry with a regulator, the question is never only whether the output is good. It is who is accountable, what was recorded, and whether the data crossed a line it should not have. Products that answer those questions convincingly get bought in sectors where products that merely work well do not.",
      "## The practical read",
      "Do not build a thinking layer. Build the system of record for something narrow, with the rules and the history included, and make it easy for any agent to reach.",
      "The reasoning will keep getting better and cheaper regardless of what you do, and you will benefit from that as a customer rather than losing to it as a competitor.",
      "See [industry knowledge is the moat](/blog/industry-knowledge-is-the-moat) for the underlying argument, [audit trails are a feature](/blog/audit-trails-are-a-feature) for the record itself, and [when the buyer is an agent](/blog/when-the-buyer-is-an-agent) for the transactional version. [How the system works](/how-it-works) covers where to start.",
    ],
